# Shell scripts in this repository are executed on Linux, by Docker and by the
# CI server. A CRLF line ending makes the kernel look for an interpreter named
# "bash\r", which fails with the famously unhelpful "no such file or
# directory" even though the file plainly exists.
#
# The repository is currently clean: every script is stored with LF. This file
# exists so it stays that way. Without it, correctness depends on each
# contributor's core.autocrlf setting, which is invisible in review and only
# announces itself once a deployment breaks.
#
# The backend Dockerfile carries a sed that strips carriage returns from its
# entrypoint, which suggests this has been a live concern before even if the
# committed content is sound today.
*.sh text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
gradlew text eol=lf

# Read by tools on the server, where CRLF is equally unwelcome.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
Jenkinsfile text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts, which genuinely do want CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets, so git never attempts line-ending translation on them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.apk binary
*.jar binary
*.keystore binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
